The shape of the XeF4 molecule is square planar. This is because the Xe atom is sp³d² hybridized, which means it has four bonding pairs (with the four fluorine atoms) and two lone pairs of electrons. The hybridization and the presence of the lone pairs determine the molecular geometry, which is square planar. The lone pairs repel the bonding pairs, causing the molecule to adopt a geometry that minimizes repulsion. 

Thus, the XeF4 molecule has a square planar shape with the four fluorine atoms arranged at the vertices of a square and the lone pairs occupying equatorial positions. 

\boxed{\text{See description above for description.}}

