The covariant derivative of a vector \( V^\mu \) in curved spacetime is given by:
\[
\nabla_\alpha V^\mu = \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\alpha} + \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\beta} \begin{bmatrix} \end{bmatrix}_{\alpha \beta}
\]
where \( \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\alpha} \) is the material derivative of \( V^\mu \) with respect to \( x^\alpha \), and \( \begin{bmatrix} \end{bmatrix}_{\alpha \beta} \) is the Jacobian matrix of the transformation from the old basis to the new basis. The term \( \frac{\partial V^\mu}{\partial x^\beta} \) is the rate of change of \( V^\mu \) with respect to \( x^\beta \), and \( \begin{bmatrix} \end{bmatrix}_{\alpha \beta} \) accounts for the non-inertainty of the basis. The covariant derivative is defined as the derivative of \( V^\mu \) with respect to \( x^\alpha \), accounting for the changes in the basis due to the motion of the system. 

\boxed{\text{The covariant derivative is defined as the derivative of } V^\mu \text{ with respect to } x^\alpha, \text{ accounting for the changes in the basis due to the motion of the system.}}

